            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

Although your house is currently quite air tight, it will become even tighter if you eliminate the 6" combustion air pipe that would no longer be required once the power-vented hot water tank is eliminated. You would still have the 4" fresh air directly connected to the return air plenum of the furnace (providing fresh outside makeup air for whenever an exhaust appliance is being used (kitchen range hood, bathroom fan or the clothes dryer). Perhaps you will choose to eliminate the 4" pipe and re-purpose the 6" combustion air to become the new fresh air into the furnace plenum ?:



Savings: There should be about a 10% reduction in overall air leakage that, because your house is energy tight already, will likely be offset by the additional ventilation you will need to provide, perhaps by operating a bathroom fan a bit more or perhaps if you choose to install a Heat Recovery Ventilation unit (see that section of my report). 



CANADA GREENER HOMES GRANT



Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, a grant of $715 is available for improving the airtightness of your home based on achieving the 10% reduction I have modeled.



An air-sealing grant is available if the airtightness of your home is improved to achieve or exceed the air change rate target proposed above. Achieving any of the targets defined for your home typically requires that the work be performed by an air-sealing professional.</airSeal>
                <mainWalls>

You have previously completed a wall insulation upgrade through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP) that was launched on April 1, 2019. There are 3 tiers of rebates associated with how much insulation is added to the above foundation exterior walls ($3,000 for adding R10 - R14, $5,000 for adding R15 - R19, and $8,000 for adding R20+ to 100% of the wall surface area). You added 4" on the Energy Wall product (R18.8 nominal) to the left &amp; right sides of your house &amp; to the upper left &amp; right pony walls of the basement, qualifying for $1,912.50 of combined wall insulation rebates.



As discussed, I will model the impact of adding the same 4" of Energy Wall product to the back wall section of the house, including the foundation pony walls below (=27% of total exposed wall area).



Savings: By adding the 4" of Energy Wall to the a minimum R20 to the back wall of the house, including the foundation pony walls below, you could potentially save $ 194 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 6.7 GJ, and qualify for $1,350 in rebates from the DHERP plus $1,344 from the Canada Greener Homes Grant.



NOTE: Please see the "Additional Comments" section of this report for important information about the DHERP, and your eligibility to participate.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



CANADA GREENER HOMES GRANT:



Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, a grant of up to $5,000 is available for adding insulation to the exterior wall area of your home. Insulation grants are based on the percentage of wall area to which the insulation has been added and the amount of insulation added. A minimum of 20% of the exterior wall area of your home, excluding foundation walls, must be insulated in order to qualify for a grant.

</mainWalls>
                <ceilings />
                <foundation />
                <windows>

If you proceed with a rear wall insulation upgrade, you may consider upgrading some of the windows with new ENERGY STARÂ® qualified windows. I recommend installing ENERGY STARÂ® qualified, full frame replacement windows that are triple pane, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged or fixed-frame windows, with a U-Value (rate of heat loss) no higher than 1.22 W/mÂ²K.



DEEP HOME ENERGY RETROFIT PROGRAM



You could qualify for up to $250 in rebates (per eligible window, up to a 12 window replacement maximum) from the DHERP.



NOTE: Please see the "Additional Comments" section of this report for important information about the DHERP, and your eligibility to participate.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



CANADA GREENER HOMES GRANT



Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, grants of either $162.50 (max 1.22 W/mÂ²K) or $325 (max 0.85 W/mÂ²K) per window rough opening are available when an ENERGY STAR certified or ENERGY STAR certified Most Efficient window is installed to replace an existing window. Replacing old, damaged or leaky windows with new ENERGY STAR certified products can help you save on energy, improve comfort and reduce noise. To determine eligibility of the product you are seeking to purchase, confirm that it is included on the list of eligible equipment as found on the Canada Greener Homes Grant webpage.</windows>
                <doors>

If you proceed with a rear wall insulation upgrade, you may consider upgrading the rear garden door unit with a new ENERGY STARÂ® qualified unit. I recommend doors with a U-Value (rate of heat loss) no higher than 1.22 W/mÂ²K or an Energy Rating of a minimum 34.



CANADA GREENER HOMES GRANT



Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, a grant of $162.50 per hinged door system (includes all windows in the door, sidelights and transom windows above) is available when an eligible ENERGY STAR certified door or door system is installed. Replacing old, damaged or leaky hinged doors with new ENERGY STAR certified products can help you save on energy and improve comfort. To determine eligibility of the product you are seeking to purchase, confirm that it is included on the list of eligible equipment as found on the Canada Greener Homes Grant webpage. Please see the Additional Comments section of this report for further information.</doors>
                <VentilationSystem>

Overall, your home is very air tight and relies on mechanical exhaust ventilation (kitchen range hood, bathroom fans &amp; the clothes dryer) that relies on the fresh air supply pipe connected to the furnace ductwork (and likely the combustion air supply pipe as well). Even with these 2 pipes, your house models as requiring additional ventilation.



If your house was built today, it would include a Heat Recovery Ventilation (HRV) unit to manage indoor air quality, protecting the home from unwanted toxins, pollutants, and odors and help protect the overall structure of a home by eliminating excess moisture from the air. An energy-efficient HRV is one of the best ways to control indoor air quality. Retrofitting an HRV into an existing house is more difficult and is usually connected to the furnace ductwork as the air distribution system.



If you were to install an HRV, I would recommend that you select an ENERGY STARÂ® rated Heat Recovery Ventilator (HRV) system, with a minimum 75% efficiency, designed and installed by an individual who holds current certification from an organization, such as HRAI.



Savings: By installing an ENERGY STARÂ® rated HRV, with a minimum 75% efficiency, this would increase the ventilation of the house, resulting in an estimated additional $ 81 per year in energy costs and increase your annual energy usage by 2.2 GJ. There are currently no rebates available for installing an HRV from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

</VentilationSystem>
                <HotWaterSystem>

You currently have a 182 litre power vented propane hot water tank that has an Energy Factor (EF) of 0.70., and you are now considering a replacement option.



There are some options for you to consider:



OPTION 1

You are thinking about installing a Heat Pump Water Heater. I modeled one of these for another Yellowknife homeowner who was purchasing one of the Rheem 50 gallon units (CPROPH50 T2 RH375-SO)  that has a Uniform Energy Factor of 3.75. There is very little first hand information in the NWT regarding their operational effectiveness, and I think there are a few things you should consider:



A. Electric hot water tanks in Yellowknife usually start to leak within 5-8 years, due to the microbial content of our domestic water supply. As the Heat Pump Water Heater is is still essentially an electric tank, it may also fail within 5 - 8 years



B. A Heat Pump Water Heater is taking heat from inside the house (air heated by your furnace) and using it to heat your hot water. The heat withdrawn from the air will place an extra load (ie: more litres of propane  use) on your furnace.



C. A Heat Pump Water Heater needs open access to heated air around it, so it cannot be tucked away behind a wall or in a sealed basement room of your house.



D. There will be some sound emitting from the Heat Pump Water Heater ........ how loud will it be and will it bother you ? Will it be any louder than the current power vented hot water tank ?



Savings: Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, a grant of $1,300 is available for installing an eligible ENERGY STAR certified domestic hot water heat pump. Heat pump water heaters transfer heat from the indoor air to the water thereby partially reducing room temperatures which will increase heating requirements somewhat during the heating season. At the same time, the equipment will remove moisture from the air while cooling it, which can be beneficial during the non-heating season - this may not be a good thing, if your house is already too dry during the winter ?



The software analyses calculates an increase in annual electricity use of 1,205 kWh @ $0.33 = $398. There is a net decrease in propane use (savings from propane DHW tank eliminated together with an increase in furnace propane consumption) of 396 litres @ $0.71 = $281, for a net annual increase in utility cost of $117.



FYI - there are many homes in Yellowknife that have an electric hot water tank that operate around 0.90 EF (about 90% of the electricity used is directly attributed to the hot water, while only 10% is radiated into the house as heat loss). I did an alternate quote for your house to determine the energy savings assuming you had an electric hot water tank. There would be 3,739 kWh ($1,234) of electricity savings, 410 litres ($291) of increased propane use for the furnace, for a net annual savings of about $943.00. So ........... there will be some homeowners in Yellowknife (with electric hot water heaters) who will consider a Heat Pump Hot Water Heater !!!



To determine eligibility of the product you are seeking to purchase, confirm that it is included on the list of eligible equipment as found on the Canada Greener Homes Grant webpage.

- https://oee.nrcan.gc.ca/pml-lmp/index.cfm?action=app.search-recherche&amp;appliance=HP_WH_GH



OPTION 2

I modeled the option for you to consider replacing your current power vented propane hot water heater with an ENERGY STARÂ® qualified instantaneous condensing propane-fired water heater with an Energy Factor of 0.95 or higher. There are a lot of on-demand propane hot water heaters in Yellowknife and (because they do not store water) they seem to last much longer ...... exceeding the 10-year warranty they come with. I believe the hot water heaters in all of the new manufactured houses along Hall Crescent (by the jail) have on-demand hot water heaters, tucked away in a closet where nobody sees or hears them.



Savings: By replacing your current hot water tank with an ENERGY STARÂ® qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with a thermal efficiency of a minimum 0.95 EF, could potentially save $ 76 per year in energy costs,  and qualify for up to $ 750 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. These savings are calculated as:

 

For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



NOTE: The GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program is regularly reviewed and the rebate amount and/or eligibility criteria can change. In addition, rebate funding is provided on a first-come, first-serve basis contingent upon available funding. Please check our website before purchasing to determine current rebate amounts, eligibility criteria and if rebate funding is still available.

</HotWaterSystem>
